From: Eduardo Ruiz Duarte <toorandom@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: ipu-bridge: hold a reference on the IVSC software node

ipu_bridge_instantiate_ivsc() attaches the IVSC software node to the mei-csi
device with set_secondary_fwnode() but never takes a reference on it, while the
driver core unconditionally drops one when that device goes away.

device_add() calls software_node_notify(), which does a kobject_get() on the
swnode behind dev_fwnode(dev). device_del() calls software_node_notify_remove(),
which does the matching kobject_put() -- and that put sits *outside* the
"if (swnode->managed)" block, so it runs for every device whose fwnode is a
software node, managed or not:

	sysfs_remove_link(&swnode->kobj, dev_name(dev));
	sysfs_remove_link(&dev->kobj, "software_node");
	kobject_put(&swnode->kobj);

	if (swnode->managed) {
		set_secondary_fwnode(dev, NULL);
		kobject_put(&swnode->kobj);
	}

csi_dev is the mei-csi device, which the MEI client bus has already added by the
time ipu_bridge_instantiate_ivsc() runs. The add-side get therefore never
happens for this node, but the del-side put always does.

On hardware where the MEI bus resets -- which it does on every s2idle and S4
resume on a Dell XPS 13 9340 -- mei-csi is destroyed and recreated each cycle,
so the node loses one reference per resume. INTC10CF-N starts at 3 (its own
registration plus one for each of its two port children, since kobject_add() of
a child takes a reference on the parent), so the third resume frees it.
software_node_find_by_name() then returns NULL and isys walks into freed memory:

  refcount_t: addition on 0; use-after-free
   kobject_get
   software_node_graph_get_port_parent
   fwnode_graph_get_port_parent
   fwnode_graph_get_endpoint_by_id
   isys_notifier_init [intel_ipu6_isys]
   isys_register_devices / isys_probe
   deferred_probe_work_func

Take a reference so the attachment owns one, balancing the put the core will do
when csi_dev is destroyed. On the path where the device is never destroyed this
leaks a single kobject reference, which is what the lifetime of the node already
assumes.

Signed-off-by: Eduardo Ruiz Duarte <toorandom@gmail.com>
---
 drivers/media/pci/intel/ipu-bridge.c | 9 +++++++++
 1 file changed, 9 insertions(+)

--- a/drivers/media/pci/intel/ipu-bridge.c
+++ b/drivers/media/pci/intel/ipu-bridge.c
@@ -672,6 +672,15 @@ static int ipu_bridge_instantiate_ivsc(struct ipu_sensor *sensor)
 	if (!fwnode)
 		return -ENODEV;
 
+	/*
+	 * Balance the kobject_put() that software_node_notify_remove() will do
+	 * on this node when csi_dev is destroyed. That put is unconditional,
+	 * while the matching get in software_node_notify() only happens at
+	 * device_add() time -- and csi_dev was added before we got here, so it
+	 * never ran for this node.
+	 */
+	fwnode_handle_get(fwnode);
+
 	set_secondary_fwnode(sensor->csi_dev, fwnode);
 
 	return 0;
